{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779418681,"nonce":"Zc5GSJgFEpE4yYdTxROxECjsYqk=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDDBZuQ","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/154","origin":"github"},"title":"Add bag → per-camera video extraction script (wall-clock-synced)","message":"## Goal\n\nA reusable script in `bizzyboat_project11/scripts/` (or shared between `bizzyboat_` and `izzyboat_`, parameterizable) that takes a ROS 2 bag and produces per-camera mp4 files with correct wall-clock-aligned PTS, plus an optional multi-camera grid render. Bakes in the sync-correctness lessons from today's manual extraction so we don't have to re-learn them next time.\n\n## Background\n\n2026-05-21: extracted four OAK camera streams (`oak_forward/aft/port/starboard`) from a 1-hour BizzyBoat bag for review. Naive approach (concatenate all `FFMPEGPacket` bytes, mux at 5 fps) produced files that *looked* synced but drifted by up to ~2.8 s between cameras, because:\n\n- Different cameras have different non-decodable startup packets (8 / 19 / 4 / 10), so the first decoded frame's wall-clock time differs per camera.\n- HEVC decoder error rates over the rest of the stream also differ (\"Too many refs in a short term RPS\" / \"No ref lists in the SPS\"), baking accumulating drift into a constant-fps mux.\n\nFix: use each frame's `header.stamp` as the encoded PTS, producing VFR mkv files whose absolute PTS matches wall-clock capture time. Then cut grids using `trim=start=…:end=…` (absolute PTS) inside `filter_complex` instead of `-ss` (which is offset-from-start on mkv with non-zero `start_time` — non-obvious gotcha worth a comment in the script).\n\nWe'll keep needing this — deployment debriefs, post-mission analysis, student review, mission documentation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Input: bag path, optional camera-topic list (default: glob `/bizzy/sensors/cameras/oak_*/image_raw/ffmpeg`), optional time range, optional output directory.\n- Output: per-camera `.mp4` with PTS = `header.stamp` of the captured frame (so any later cuts are wall-clock-accurate by default).\n- Optional grid render with configurable layout (start with the 2×3 rearview-mirror layout we built today).\n- Default output path: `~/data/logs/analysis/\u003cbag-date\u003e_camera_extract/` per the workspace analysis-artifact convention.\n- Codec: re-encode HEVC packets to H.264 for broad player compatibility; consider stream-copy mode for fast extraction when sync is already good (e.g., short snippets).\n- Initial transport support: `ffmpeg_image_transport_msgs/FFMPEGPacket`. Stretch: `sensor_msgs/CompressedImage` and raw `sensor_msgs/Image`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Horizon stabilization — tracked in [rolker/image_warper#1](https://github.com/rolker/image_warper/issues/1).\n- Multi-camera stitching / panorama — also image_warper.\n- Live ROS 2 processing — also image_warper.\n\n## Future\n\nOnce `image_warper` (jazzy) has its Python prototype landed under [#1](https://github.com/rolker/image_warper/issues/1), that's probably the right long-term home for this — the script collapses to a CLI front-end on shared library code, and the stabilization/stitching features extend naturally. This issue is for the interim utility AND a reference implementation for the image_warper port.\n\n## Done when\n\n- Script + invocation docs land via PR on `jazzy`.\n- Re-running it on today's `bag_2026-05-21T13.25.26_ffmpeg_seg` reproduces something equivalent to `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-05-21_camera_extract_synced/grid_rearview_29-40_to_30-00_synced_v4.mp4` (4-camera, 2×3 rearview grid, wall-clock-synced).\n\n## Reference\n\n- Synced per-camera mkv files from today: `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-05-21_camera_extract_synced/`\n- Working ffmpeg recipe (the trim-filter cut) in this issue's discussion can be lifted as the starting point.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}